  card="A tissue that connects and supports other tissues is called a connective tissue. Blood and bones are both connective tissues, but they differ in composition and consistency: blood is fluid while bone is hard, a difference due to the matrix, which is soft and jelly-like in blood but hard, solid and rigid in bones. The red colour of blood is due to haemoglobin, an iron-rich protein in the Red Blood Cells (RBCs), which live for about 4 months and are replaced regularly. Platelets help in blood clotting at the site of an injury. During exercise muscles need more oxygen, so breathing becomes faster and blood flow increases. White Blood Cells (WBCs) collect at infected areas, causing pus formation and inflammation.",
